 276:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****   /*
 277:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * In library releases up through v6a, we called jpeg_abort() here to free
 278:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * any working memory allocated by the destination manager and marker
 279:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * writer.  Some applications had a problem with that: they allocated space
 280:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * of their own from the library memory manager, and didn't want it to go
 281:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * away during write_tables.  So now we do nothing.  This will cause a
 282:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * memory leak if an app calls write_tables repeatedly without doing a full
 283:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * compression cycle or otherwise resetting the JPEG object.  However, that
 284:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * seems less bad than unexpectedly freeing memory in the normal case.
 285:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * An app that prefers the old behavior can call jpeg_abort for itself after
 286:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    * each call to jpeg_write_tables().
 287:Middlewares/Third_Party/LibJPEG/source/jcapimin.c ****    */
